## 2. Five Mandatory Analytical Limitations

### 2.1 County-Level (Ecological) Deprivation vs Student-Level Deprivation
* **What We Measure:** Step 3 links HEA Irish-domiciled enrolments by home county (`{{national_county_mapped_enrol_2025_26}}` mapped students in `2025/2026`, after quarantining `{{silver_quarantined_county_students_2025_26}}` `Unknown Ireland` / `N/A` records) to the population-weighted Pobal HP 2022 relative deprivation index across **`{{pobal_ed_count}}`** Electoral Divisions in **`{{n_counties}}`** counties.
* **Why Ecological Inference Must Not Be Overstated:** Every county contains both affluent and disadvantaged Electoral Divisions and Small Areas. For example, **Dublin** has a net affluent county-wide population-weighted score (**`{{cty_dublin_rel_score}}`**), yet **`{{cty_dublin_disadv_share}}`** of Dublin's **`{{cty_dublin_pop22}}`** residents live in Electoral Divisions classified as disadvantaged (`Index22_ED_rel_wt_cat` in categories `1` to `3`). Conversely, a student domiciled in a county with a negative mean Pobal score (such as **Longford** at **`{{cty_longford_rel_score}}`**, **Wexford** at **`{{cty_wexford_rel_score}}`**, **Cavan** at **`{{cty_cavan_rel_score}}`**, or **Offaly** at **`{{cty_offaly_rel_score}}`**) may personally come from an affluent household.
* **Policy Implication:** County-level analysis is highly informative for **regional outreach planning, school-link geography, and commuter catchment monitoring**, whereas **NAP Goal 1 target tracking** (raising the disadvantaged-area transition rate from **`{{nap_disadv_transition_base}}`** to **`{{nap_disadv_transition_target}}`**) requires Small Area Eircode geocoding inside the institutional student record system.

### 2.2 Absence of Disability, Ethnicity, and Socio-Economic Entry Route Flags in Public SRS Exports
* The public HEA `Access our Data - Students` interactive download tool publishes aggregate headcount cross-tabulations across nine structural dimensions (`Institute`, `Programme Type`, `Age Group`, `Mode of Study`, `Field of Study`, `Course Level`, `Domicile Group`, `Gender`, and Irish-domiciled `County`).
* It does **not** include disability category, DARE/HEAR eligibility or offer acceptance, DEIS post-primary school attendance, QQI/FET entry basis, socio-economic group, or Irish Traveller and Roma ethnicity. Those variables are collected separately through the **HEA Equal Access Survey (EAS)** and institutional Access Office registrations.

### 2.3 Nearest-`{{hea_rounding_base}}` Rounding and Small-Cell Suppression (`<{{small_cell_threshold}}`)
* **Published Rounding:** All values in the HEA `Access our Data - Students` exports are independently rounded to the nearest **`{{hea_rounding_base}}`**. When summing atomic cells across a dimension (`Programme Type`, `Age Group`, `Mode of Study`, `Field of Study`, `Course Level`), independent rounding creates minor additive differences up to **`{{dq_max_rounding_diff}}`** students compared with the published `Row_Total` (`{{dq_reconciled_tables}}` institution-year-dimension tables reconcile within `10` students).
* **GDPR-Style Small-Cell Suppression Rule:** In accordance with statistical disclosure control practice under GDPR and HEA reporting standards, AccessLens-IE flags and suppresses all non-zero cells strictly below **`{{small_cell_threshold}}`** (i.e., published cells equal to **`{{hea_rounding_base}}`**) as **`"<10"`** in output display fields (`{{suppressed_inst_dim_cells}}` institution-dimension cells and `{{suppressed_county_inst_cells}}` county-institution flow cells, totalling **`{{suppressed_total_cells}}`** suppressed cells across the 7-year series).

### 2.4 Institutional Scope & Structural Changes (`2019/2020` to `2025/2026`)
* The dataset covers the **`{{n_hea_institutions}}`** HEA-funded higher education institutions reporting to the SRS. Private/independent higher education providers not funded by the HEA are outside the scope of these tables.
* **National College of Ireland (NCI)** entered the HEA SRS reporting dataset in `2023/2024`; its **`{{dq_nci_pre_scope_years}}`** prior academic years (`2019/2020` to `2022/2023`) appear as `0` in raw multi-year exports and are explicitly flagged with `in_hea_scope = FALSE` in `gold.fact_institution_year` so pre-2023/2024 institutional averages are not distorted.
* **St. Angela's College** integrated with **Atlantic Technological University (ATU)** during the period, with programme transfers reflected in the later years of the `2019/2020` to `2025/2026` series.

### 2.5 Observational Data Only (No Causal Claims)
* All statistical relationships reported in AccessLens-IE (including the county-level correlation between Pobal HP relative affluence/deprivation score and higher education participation per `1,000` residents, $r = {{corr_rel_2025_26_r}}$) are **descriptive and associative**. Higher county participation rates in counties such as **Galway (`{{cty_galway_rate_1k_2025_26}}` per `1,000`)**, **Cork (`{{cty_cork_rate_1k_2025_26}}` per `1,000`)**, **Sligo (`{{cty_sligo_rate_1k_2025_26}}` per `1,000`)**, and **Kildare (`{{cty_kildare_rate_1k_2025_26}}` per `1,000`)** reflect a combination of socio-economic composition, secondary school attainment (`EDHIGH22` correlation $r = {{corr_edhigh_2025_26_r}}$), age structure, and physical proximity to university and technological university campuses.
